# Playwright conventions

Illustrative UI names only — confirm real BuddyTime copy from the live app before shipping locators.

**At a glance:** (1) Role-first locators. (2) Web-first waiting only. (3) POMs = actions, no asserts. (4) Setup-project auth + `storageState`. (5) Unique, self-cleaning data. (6) One tag per `test()`. (7) Factories, enums, env. (8) API for arrange/teardown. (9) Mock dependencies, not the SUT. (10) Axe + keyboard on key flows. (11) Strict expects; soft/screenshot when needed. (12) Fix flake at the source, not with retries/workers.

## 1. Locators

Prefer locators that mirror what users see and do; reserve test IDs for when nothing user-facing is stable.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save availability' }).click();
await page.getByLabel('Child nickname').fill(nickname);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator('//div[@class="btn-primary"]').click();
await page.locator('#child-nickname-input').fill(nickname);
```

**Why:** Role- and label-based locators survive layout and styling changes; CSS/XPath ties tests to implementation detail.

## 2. Waiting

Let Playwright auto-wait via assertions; never sleep or snapshot visibility into a boolean assert.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
await expect.poll(async () => list.count()).toBe(3);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(2000);
expect(await row.isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Fixed delays flake; web-first `expect` retries until the UI is ready or fails with a useful timeout.

## 3. Page Object Model

One class per page or component; expose actions, not expectations.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
export class AvailabilityPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {
    this.saveButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save availability' });
  }
  private readonly saveButton: Locator;

  /** Saves the weekly grid for the current family. */
  async save(): Promise<void> {
    await this.saveButton.click();
  }
}
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
async saveAndAssert(page: Page): Promise<void> {
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save availability' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByText('Saved')).toBeVisible();
}
```

**Why:** POMs keep specs readable and reuse flows; assertions belong in tests so failures point at the scenario.

## 4. Auth

Authenticate once per family in the setup project; reuse `storageState` for all app tests.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
// auth.setup.ts — credentials from process.env only
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.APP_USER_EMAIL!);
await context.storageState({ path: AUTH_FILE });

// two-family spec
const alt = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.APP_USER_EMAIL!);
});
```

**Why:** Login per test is slow and brittle; setup + `storageState` matches Playwright’s recommended auth pattern.

## 5. Test data

Create unique entities per test and tear them down so parallel runs and shared envs stay safe.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
const familyName = `Family-${Date.now()}`;
const family = await familyFactory.create(request, { name: familyName });
try {
  // test body
} finally {
  await familyFactory.delete(request, family.id);
}
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('uses shared playdate', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/playdates/12345'); // fixed id from another run
});
```

**Why:** Shared or leftover data causes order-dependent failures and collisions in the BuddyTime test environment.

## 6. Tags

Exactly one tag on each `test()`; choose the narrowest suite that owns the scenario.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('shows empty state when no friends', { tag: '@smoke' }, async ({ page }) => {
  // ...
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe('Availability', { tag: '@regression' }, () => {
  test('grid loads', async ({ page }) => { /* ... */ });
});
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI selection; `@destructive` is only for shared global state (run with `--workers=1`), and mutating tests must revert in `afterEach`/`afterAll`.

## 7. Test data sources

Centralize builders, static invalid sets, env secrets, and repeated strings.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import { buildChildProfile } from '../test-data/factories/child.factory';
import { AppRoute } from '../test-data/routes';

await page.goto(AppRoute.Availability);
const child = buildChildProfile();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.goto('https://test.example.app/availability');
await page.getByLabel('Name').fill('Test Child');
```

**Why:** Factories and enums keep specs DRY; env vars keep secrets out of source control.

## 8. API

Use `request` for arrange/act/assert on APIs; UI is for what only the UI can validate.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
const res = await request.post('/api/families', { data: buildFamilyPayload() });
expect(res.ok()).toBeTruthy();
await page.goto(AppRoute.Dashboard);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.goto('/onboarding');
// 12 UI steps to create a family before the assertion under test
```

**Why:** API setup is faster and stabilizes tests around the behavior you actually care about.

## 9. Network mocking

Simulate failure and edge responses on dependency routes, not the endpoint under test.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/notifications/**', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: '{}' }),
);
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Refresh' }).click();
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toContainText('Try again');
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/availability/**', (route) =>
  route.fulfill({ json: { slots: [] } }),
); // endpoint under test
```

**Why:** Mocking the SUT hides real integration bugs; mock upstream failures to test resilience.

## 10. Accessibility

Automated WCAG scans plus keyboard paths; role locators double as name checks.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('main')).toBeVisible();
await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Get started' })).toBeFocused();
const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).analyze();
expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.click('.hero-cta'); // no role, no keyboard check, no axe
```

**Why:** BuddyTime serves families; accessible names and keyboard flows catch regressions unit tests miss.

## 11. Assertions

Default to strict web-first checks; use soft asserts and screenshots deliberately.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('status')).toHaveText('Invite sent');
await expect.soft(card).toHaveScreenshot({ mask: [page.locator('[data-dynamic-date]')] });
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const text = await page.locator('.status').textContent();
expect(text).toContain('sent');
// expect removed because flaky
```

**Why:** Locator-based expects retry; weakening or deleting assertions hides product bugs.

## 12. Anti-flake

Fix root causes — isolation, clocks, locale — instead of cranking retries or serial workers.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-09-15T10:00:00-04:00') });
// playwright.config: locale en-CA, timezoneId America/Toronto, retries only in CI
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe.configure({ retries: 5 });
// or workers: 1 to "fix" shared state without cleanup
```

**Why:** Retries and single-worker masks data leaks; pinned time and locale match BuddyTime’s Canadian scheduling context.

## Refusals (hard stops)

Never weaken or delete an assertion to pass; never heal a real bug; never replace a role locator with CSS/XPath; never use `waitForTimeout`, a hardcoded credential, or the `any` type. Each one is a conflict the agent raises, not a preference it can be talked out of.
